#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:106
#: ../content/processes-automation/web-services.rst:134
msgid "Like comment, but longer text can be added here."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:109
msgid "Stop after match"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:109
msgid "ACLs are evaluated in alphabetical order. This setting disables the evaluation of the subsequent ACLs."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:116
msgid "Edit ACL Structure"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:118
msgid "The ACL definition can be split into two big parts, *Match settings* and *Change settings*. In the matching sections the ACLs contain attributes that has to be met in order to use the ACL. If the attributes defined in the ACL do not match with the attributes that are sent, then the ACL does not take any affect, but any other match ACL will. The change sections contain the rules to reduce the possible options for a ticket."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:122
msgid "Match settings"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:125
msgid "``Properties``"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:125
msgid "This section contains matching options that can be changed on the fly. For example on a ticket creation time the data of the ticket changes dynamically as the agent sets the information. If an ACL is set to match a ticket attribute then only when the matching attribute is selected the ACL will be active and might reduce other ticket attributes, but as soon as another value is selected the ACL will not take any affect."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:129
msgid "``PropertiesDatabase``"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:128
msgid "This section is similar to ``Properties`` but does not take changes in ticket attributes that are not saved into the database, this means that changing an attribute without submit will not make any effect. This section is not use for ticket creation screens (as tickets are not yet created in the database)."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:132
msgid "Change settings"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:135
msgid "``Possible``"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:135
msgid "This section is used to reset the data to be reduce to only the elements that are set in this section."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:138
msgid "``PossibleAdd``"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:138
msgid "This section is used to add missing elements that were reduced in other ACLs. This section is only used in together with other ACLs that have ``Possible`` or ``PossibleNot`` sections."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:142
msgid "``PossibleNot``"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:141
msgid "This section is used to remove specific elements from the current data. It could be used stand alone or together with other ACLs with a ``Possible`` or ``PossibleAdd`` sections."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:145
msgid "Modifiers"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:147
msgid "In order to make the development of ACLs easier and more powerful there is a set of so called modifiers for the attributes on each section. This modifiers are explained below:"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:150
msgid "``[Not]``"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:150
msgid "This modifier is used to negate a value, for example ``[Not]2 low``. Talking about priorities will be the same as to have: *1 very low*, *3 normal*, *4 high*, *5 very high*."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:153
msgid "``[RegExp]``"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:153
msgid "It is used to define a regular expression for matching several values, for example ``[RegExp]low``. In this case talking about priorities is the same as *1 very low*, *2 low*."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:156
msgid "``[regexp]``"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:156
msgid "It is very similar to ``[RegExp]`` but it is case insensitive."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:159
msgid "``[NotRegExp]``"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:159
msgid "Negated regular expressions, for example ``[NotRegExp]low``. Talking about priorities is the same as *3 normal*, *4 high*, *5 very high*."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:163
msgid "``[Notregexp]``"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:162
msgid "It is very similar to ``[NotRegExp]`` but it is case insensitive."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:166
msgid "ACL Examples"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:170
msgid "Move Ticket to Queue Based on Priority"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:172
msgid "This example shows you how to allow movement into a queue of only those tickets with ticket priority *5 very high*."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:174
msgid "First, it needs to have a name. In this case, it is *100-Example-ACL*. Note that the ACLs will be numerically sorted before execution, so you should use the names carefully. The comment and the description fields are optional."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:179
msgid "100-Example-ACL - Basic Settings"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:181
msgid "Secondly, you have a *Properties* section which is a filter for your tickets. All the criteria defined here will be applied to a ticket to determine if the ACL must be applied or not. In our example, a ticket will match if it is in the queue *Raw* and has priority *5 very high*. This is also affected by changes in the form (e. g. if the ticket is the queue *Raw* and had a priority *3 normal* at this moment the ACL will not match, but then priority drop-down is selected and the priority is changed now to *5 very high* then will also match)."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:186
msgid "100-Example-ACL - Match Settings"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:188
msgid "Lastly, a section *Possible* defines modifications to the screens. In this case, from the available queues, only the queue *Alert* can be selected in a ticket screen."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:193
msgid "100-Example-ACL - Change Settings"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:197
msgid "Don't forget to set *Validity* to *valid* and deploy the newly created ACL."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:201
msgid "Move Ticket to Queue Based on Priority Stored in Database"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:203
msgid "This example is very similar to the first one, but in this case only tickets in the queue *Raw* and with a priority *5 very high*, both stored in the database will match. This kind of ACLs does not consider changes in the form before the ticket is really updated in the database."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:208
msgid "101-Example-ACL"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:212
msgid "Disable Ticket Close in Queue and Hide Close Button"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:214
msgid "This example shows how to disable the closing of tickets in the queue *Raw*, and hide the close button. It is possible to filter a ticket field (state) with more than one possible values to select from. It is also possible to limit the actions that can be executed for a certain ticket. In this case, the ticket cannot be closed."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:219
msgid "102-Example-ACL"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:223
msgid "Remove State"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:225
msgid "This example shows how it is possible to define negative filters (the state *closed successful* will be removed). You can also see that not defining match properties for a ticket will match any ticket, i. e. the ACL will always be applied. This may be useful if you want to hide certain values by default, and only enable them in special circumstances (e. g. if the agent is in a specific group)."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:230
msgid "103-Example-ACL"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:234
msgid "Using Regular Expressions"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:236
msgid "This example shows you how you can use regular expressions for matching tickets and for filtering the available options. This ACL only shows *Hardware* services for tickets that are created in queues that start with *HW*."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:241
msgid "104-Example-ACL"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:245
msgid "Disallow Process For Customer"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:247
msgid "This ACL restricts the process *P14* in the external interface using the customer ID *TheCustomerID*."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:252
msgid "105-Example-ACL"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:256
msgid "Disallow Standard Reply Function For Customers in Process"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/access-control-lists.rst:258
msgid "This ACL prohibits using the reply button in CustomerTicketZoom if the ticket is a process ticket of the process with entity ID *Process-0123456789abcdef*."
msgstr ""

