#: ../content/processes-automation/process-management.rst:208
msgid "Processes are more complex than other resources in OTOBO. To create a process, you need to do several steps. The following chapters show you, how to define a process from the specification and create the needed resources. Let's see an example to make it more demonstrative. We will define a book order process."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/process-management.rst:212
msgid "Process Specification"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/process-management.rst:214
msgid "The book order process has four states."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/process-management.rst:223
msgid "Recording the demand"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/process-management.rst:217
msgid "Before an order will be placed, the demand for literature by an employee will be recorded. The following book is needed in our example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/process-management.rst:226
msgid "Approval by manager"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/process-management.rst:226
msgid "The head of the employee's department needs to decide on the order. In case of a denial, a reason should be recorded by the manager. In case of approval, the order is passed to the purchasing department."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/process-management.rst:229
msgid "Processing by purchasing department"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/process-management.rst:229
msgid "Purchasing now has the task to find out where the book can be ordered with the best conditions. If it is out of stock, this can be recorded in the order. In case of a successful order purchasing will record the supplier, the price and the delivery date."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/process-management.rst:233
msgid "Processing by the mail room"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/process-management.rst:232
msgid "The shipment will arrive at the company. The incoming goods department checks the shipment and records the date of receipt. Now the employee will be informed that their order has arrived and is ready to be collected."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/process-management.rst:236
msgid "Introduce The Process Elements"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/process-management.rst:238
msgid "If we assume that a ticket acts in this workflow like an accompanying document that can receive change notes, we already have a clear picture of process tickets."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/process-management.rst:240
msgid "From the analysis of the example process we can identify the following necessary items:"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/process-management.rst:242
msgid "Possibility to record data, let's call this :term:`activity dialog`."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/process-management.rst:243
msgid "Check which can react to changed data automatically, let's call this :term:`transition`."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/process-management.rst:244
msgid "Change which can be applied to a process ticket after successful transitions of a process ticket, let's call this :term:`transition action`."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/process-management.rst:245
msgid "A possibility to offer more than just one activity dialog to be available. In our example this is needed when the manager must have the choice between *Approve* and *Deny*. Let's call this :term:`activity`."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/processes-automation/process-management.rst:247
